WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.250
STEPHANIE WONG: Hey, everyone.

00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:03.510
This is Stephanie Wong,
Cloud Developer Advocate.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:06.090
And today, I am
really, really excited,

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:09.390
because we have our Google
Fellow here, Eric Brewer.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.480
He has been here helping to
build Google and Google Cloud's

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.120
infrastructure over
the last like decade.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.460
And we are so honored
to have him here today.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.810
So Eric, thank you so
much for being here.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:20.983
How are you today?

00:00:20.983 --> 00:00:22.650
ERIC BREWER: I am
fantastic, hanging out

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:25.110
in wonderful Marin
County, enjoying

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.460
being remote for today.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:27.543
STEPHANIE WONG: Same here.

00:00:27.543 --> 00:00:29.790
I am just a stone's
throw away from you.

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.310
But I do want to jump
into it right away.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.890
I was actually able
to dig up this tweet

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:36.390
that you had a while ago.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:38.470
I think it was
about 10 years ago.

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:40.620
And you were talking
about your initial plan

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.020
to help build infrastructure
here at Google

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.780
and how much work
there was left to do.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.890
So what was that initial
plan, and how did it pan out?

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:52.110
ERIC BREWER: Well, that
was based on my thinking

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.400
really from the '90s.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.733
So before VMs were
a big deal, I was

00:00:55.733 --> 00:00:57.150
working on what
you would now call

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.580
cloud computing at UC Berkeley.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.980
And it was really
based on a model

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.730
that used a lot of processes,
and services, and APIs.

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:07.600
And I still like
that model better.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.180
So when I got to
Google, I kind of

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.220
felt like we need
a new cloud that's

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.140
a higher level of abstraction.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.380
And of course, that led to
Kubernetes and all the things

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.722
we do in the modern
version of that.

00:01:18.722 --> 00:01:21.180
STEPHANIE WONG: What's it like
to see that come to fruition

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.980
and have the industry
adopt it over time?

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:25.680
ERIC BREWER: It's
incredibly exciting.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.380
Even when you plan
something-- like, oh,

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.033
we should do a new orchestration
layer based on containers,

00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:34.450
because it's more like how we
think the cloud should work,

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:37.290
it's more like how
Google works internally--

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:38.400
that's fine to say that.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.958
But then you have to
create the projects.

00:01:40.958 --> 00:01:42.750
And you have to decide
to open source them.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.490
You have to get
fellow travelers,

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:48.210
as I say, other groups that
want to bet on this journey.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:49.950
And of course, you
need to get adoption.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.450
And all that happened.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.900
And after maybe eight years
working on Kubernetes,

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.562
it's in a remarkable place.

00:01:56.562 --> 00:01:58.770
STEPHANIE WONG: And it's
taken quite some time for us

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.640
to get to where we are today.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.280
I'm curious, what
was it like to be

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.940
one of the first and biggest
supporters of Kubernetes,

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:08.280
working on it from
a very early stage?

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.169
Were there any
unexpected challenges

00:02:10.169 --> 00:02:13.110
that you underwent in
implementing it across Google

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:14.335
and also across the industry?

00:02:14.335 --> 00:02:16.710
ERIC BREWER: Well, I think
there were two obvious hurdles

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:17.520
internally.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:21.210
The first was we already
had App Engine and VMs.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.670
Why do we need a third
way to do computing?

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:26.180
And so there's some natural
resistance to change.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:27.930
And the answer is
because the third way is

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:30.330
better than either one alone.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:32.980
And the second one is,
this is such a big deal.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:34.470
Why would we open source it?

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:38.280
And we didn't tend to do
much open sourcing of systems

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.050
things, as we do now.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:42.330
That's a bit of
change in philosophy.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:43.950
But for Kubernetes,
I just felt it

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.410
was too big a change
for the industry for us

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.060
to do it by ourselves,
and open source

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.268
was an important part of
getting the fellow travelers

00:02:50.268 --> 00:02:50.910
that we needed.

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:51.743
STEPHANIE WONG: Yes.

00:02:51.743 --> 00:02:54.900
Yes, and it's had quite an
impact, as we know today.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.440
But tell me a little bit
more about the open source

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.990
ecosystem, what
it's grown to be,

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.660
and what it's looking like
over the next 10 years.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.030
Is the cloud going to
continue to change?

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:09.750
Are we moving towards more
higher level of abstractions

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.940
or opinionated surfaces?

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.460
ERIC BREWER: Well,
yes, yes, and yes.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:18.030
So Kubernetes is about, I think,
the second-largest open-source

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:22.030
project now behind Linux,
which makes a lot of sense

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:23.880
and is an achievement in itself.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:27.180
But really, it's because
it's now the cloud platform,

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:28.830
or the enterprise platform.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:30.880
And the reason for that
is it's both versatile--

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.120
you can do many things
with it-- but also,

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.680
that it's easier to operate than
just the raw VMs or raw disks.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.810
And so it's a good
platform to build on.

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.730
But again, I think
it's better viewed

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:44.270
as a platform for platforms.

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:46.020
If you say, well, I
really like Cloud Run.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:47.520
It's easier to use
than Kubernetes--

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.200
that is absolutely true.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:52.140
Cloud Run is not as general
purpose as Kubernetes.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:53.700
So the way I think
about that is,

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:58.500
I want many opinionated things
that are vertically oriented

00:03:58.500 --> 00:03:59.583
on top of Kubernetes.

00:03:59.583 --> 00:04:01.500
Kubernetes is great for
building those things,

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.570
both inside the
enterprise and out.

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:05.640
And in the end,
the collection will

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.430
be Kubernetes becomes less
visible, because you're

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:11.520
using the things on top of
it in your daily experience

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.507
with your future cloud.

00:04:13.507 --> 00:04:14.340
STEPHANIE WONG: Yes.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:16.649
And what I love
about the cloud space

00:04:16.649 --> 00:04:19.529
is that it's all about
providing both developer

00:04:19.529 --> 00:04:22.470
freedom and flexibility,
but also empowering

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:26.490
them to have that optionality
for these opinionated services.

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:28.650
They want cloud
providers to give them

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:30.552
the best-of-breed
security, for example.

00:04:30.552 --> 00:04:31.260
ERIC BREWER: Yes.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:32.640
It's actually good
that there are

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.550
many functions as a service
built on top of Kubernetes.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:37.340
And some will be great.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:38.340
Some will be less great.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:39.923
But the fact that
there's many of them

00:04:39.923 --> 00:04:41.580
is actually a good
thing for everybody.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:42.600
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:45.870
Now, OK, you've done 10
years of this type of work.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:49.300
It's changed at a speed that
I'm sure nobody had expected.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:52.590
But what are you hoping to
achieve over the next 10 years?

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:55.350
Anything that you might
predict will be the next wave

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:57.160
or fundamental shift?

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:58.590
Or is it really
hard to say based

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:00.337
on your experience so far?

00:05:00.337 --> 00:05:01.920
ERIC BREWER: Well,
I think one big one

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:03.750
that we're in the
middle of is how

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:06.420
to combine the philosophy
you can get from something

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:11.070
like Kubernetes and CI/CD
with the security we clearly

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:15.810
need as a nation, and in
general, for all nations.

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:19.320
And so a classic view
of security is to say,

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:22.260
oh, I don't want
to change anything.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:23.580
There might be vulnerabilities.

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:25.620
But it turns out it's
the exact opposite.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:27.120
If you really want
to be secure, you

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.520
have to be able to update
your software quickly,

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:31.800
because there are latent
security problems that

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.360
will be discovered and you'll
then need to fix them quickly.

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:36.840
And so my biggest
focus at the moment

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:40.860
is really around open-source
security, the supply chains

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:42.900
and its security, and
how to really make

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:46.590
that a much more opinionated
stretch, from source code

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:48.960
to actual deployment,
having better metadata,

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.080
better control of all those
steps, better attestations,

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:56.390
which can make proof statements
about those deliverables.

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:58.560
And so you'll understand
what your real risks are,

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:01.530
and of course, minimize as
many attack points as possible.

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:04.042
And that is going to
be a big change, also.

00:06:04.042 --> 00:06:06.000
STEPHANIE WONG: Well,
that is a perfect tee up,

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.520
because in our next video
that we're doing together,

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.430
we are going to be talking more
about how you have been helping

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:14.610
to build a better security
process for software supply

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:18.910
chains and how trusted software
providers are the future.

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:20.850
So thank you so much, Eric.

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:22.560
Everyone else, stay tuned.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:25.170
We are going to keep these
conversations coming at you.

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:28.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

